From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Subject: [PATCH] nss_phys_if: do not answer a request with the previous one's response

Every physical-interface control message - open, close, mac_addr,
change_mtu, link_state, and the interface-base messages that go through
the same path - is sent by nss_phys_if_msg_sync(), which serialises
callers on one semaphore and then waits on a single file-static
completion for the reply.

The completion is initialised once, at first use, and never reset. On
the 3 second timeout the sender abandons its request and reports
NSS_TX_FAILURE, but the request is still queued to the firmware: when
the reply eventually arrives, nss_phys_if_callback() writes the shared
response and calls complete() with nobody waiting, leaving the
completion signalled. From that point the path is permanently skewed by
one - the next sender's wait_for_completion_timeout() returns
immediately, consuming that leftover token without waiting for its own
reply, and reads the previous request's result out of the equally shared
response field. Its own reply then re-signals the completion, so the
skew sustains itself for as long as the module is loaded. Nothing short
of a reboot restores it, and none of it is visible: the driver builds
with debug messages compiled out.

The effect on the caller is that a control message reports success it
never had. A port whose link-up notification is reported as delivered,
but which the firmware never applied, is left with the host convinced
the port can transmit while the firmware silently discards everything
sent to it.

Give each request a serial, stamp it into the message's app_data so the
firmware echoes it back, and drop responses in the callback that are no
longer the one being waited for. Clear the completion before sending as
well, so a request never starts against a leftover token.

Count both events and expose them as module parameters. The messages
beside them compile out at the default debug level, which is the reason
this went unseen; a box that has never timed out reads zero on both, so
the counters also say when this is not what went wrong.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

--- a/nss_phys_if.c
+++ b/nss_phys_if.c
@@ -41,11 +41,27 @@ static struct nss_phys_if_pvt {
 	struct semaphore sem;
 	struct completion complete;
 	int response;
+	nss_ptr_t serial;
 } phif;
 
 static int nss_phys_if_sem_init_done;
 
 /*
+ * Both counters stay zero on a healthy box. A nonzero timeout count is the
+ * event that used to corrupt every later request; a nonzero stale count is this
+ * driver discarding a response that arrived too late to be the awaited one.
+ * They are module parameters because the debug messages beside them compile out
+ * at the default debug level, which is why this failure was never visible.
+ */
+static unsigned int phif_timeouts;
+module_param(phif_timeouts, uint, S_IRUGO);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(phif_timeouts, "phys_if synchronous messages that timed out");
+
+static unsigned int phif_stale_responses;
+module_param(phif_stale_responses, uint, S_IRUGO);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(phif_stale_responses, "phys_if responses discarded as stale");
+
+/*
  * nss_phys_if_update_driver_stats()
  *	Snoop the extended message and update driver statistics.
  */
@@ -145,6 +161,23 @@ static void nss_phys_if_msg_handler(stru
  */
 static void nss_phys_if_callback(void *app_data, struct nss_phys_if_msg *nim)
 {
+	/*
+	 * A request that timed out is abandoned by its sender but stays in
+	 * flight, so its response can arrive after the next request has already
+	 * been sent. Answering that one with this response would report the
+	 * wrong result for it and, worse, leave the completion signalled with
+	 * no waiter, after which every later request returns immediately with
+	 * its predecessor's status - permanently, because nothing resets it.
+	 * The serial is stamped into app_data by the sender, so a response that
+	 * is no longer the one being waited for is simply dropped here.
+	 */
+	if ((nss_ptr_t)app_data != phif.serial) {
+		phif_stale_responses++;
+		nss_warning("phys_if stale response for serial %px, current %px\n",
+				app_data, (void *)phif.serial);
+		return;
+	}
+
 	if(nim->cm.response != NSS_CMN_RESPONSE_ACK) {
 		nss_warning("phys_if Error response %d\n", nim->cm.response);
 		phif.response = NSS_TX_FAILURE;
@@ -225,6 +258,15 @@ nss_tx_status_t nss_phys_if_msg_sync(str
 
 	down(&phif.sem);
 
+	/*
+	 * Identify this request and start from a cleared completion, so that a
+	 * late response belonging to an earlier, timed-out request cannot be
+	 * mistaken for this one's.
+	 */
+	phif.serial++;
+	nim->cm.app_data = phif.serial;
+	reinit_completion(&phif.complete);
+
 	status = nss_phys_if_msg(nss_ctx, nim);
 	if(status != NSS_TX_SUCCESS)
 	{
@@ -237,6 +279,7 @@ nss_tx_status_t nss_phys_if_msg_sync(str
 
 	if(!ret)
 	{
+		phif_timeouts++;
 		nss_warning("%px: phys_if tx failed due to timeout\n", nss_ctx);
 		phif.response = NSS_TX_FAILURE;
 	}
